---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Investment Banking | Hayden Smith" description="Why investment banking, why TMT, and why Recalc.">
	<section class="wrap intro">
		<a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; Back to the beach</a>
		<p class="eyebrow">Investment Banking</p>
		<h1>Where I want to go next.</h1>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap body">
		<p class="big">
			Research taught me how to form a view. Investment banking is where a view gets attached to a client, a price, and a deadline, and has to hold up in a room with people who have done this a hundred times.
		</p>

		<p>
			I learned to build a valuation before anyone taught me how. The Rocket Lab DCF was a blank spreadsheet and a set of filings, and the hard part was never the mechanics. It was deciding which assumptions deserved to survive contact with an audience that wanted to poke holes in them. Covering RDDT and FORM for the Tartan Student Fund has been the same exercise at higher frequency: figure out what a business is actually worth when the comps don't fit, and be honest about the part of the answer you are least sure of.
		</p>

		<p>
			What I want from banking is the version of that work where it matters. Real transactions, real counterparties, and the mechanics of a deal learned from people who are exceptional at it. I have chosen the harder version of things enough times to know it suits me: I added a statistics major on top of a finance concentration, sat the IB Academy's bank-style interview process, and carried a 3.9 while working restaurant jobs through school. That last one is not filler on a résumé. Running a station through a 200-order night or coordinating 14 people across front and back of house is the same underlying discipline as a live deal, which is executing accurately at speed while other people are depending on your handoff.
		</p>

		<p>
			The sector I keep coming back to is TMT, and that is specifically because of the two names I have covered. Reddit and FormFactor sit at opposite ends of the same sector, one a consumer internet platform still working out its monetization model and the other a semiconductor test and measurement supplier selling into everybody else's roadmap. Building a variant view on both taught me that TMT is where a change in the business model shows up in the numbers first, before the narrative catches up. It is also where transaction activity concentrates, which is what connects the sector interest to the seat I want.
		</p>

		<p>
			That is also why I applied to Carnegie Mellon's Investment Banking Academy, a mentee program that runs its interview process like the banks it prepares you for. I was selected out of a pool of 50+ undergraduates. The weekly workshops cover what doesn't show up until you are doing the job: accounting and financial statement analysis, building a DCF and a merger model from a blank sheet, then defending both to someone who has sat on the other side of the table. I would rather be the least experienced person in a room that is actually doing the work than the most experienced person in a room that isn't.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap body recalc">
		<h2>Why Recalc</h2>

		<p>
			Recalc screens for work ethic rather than pedigree, and that is not a box I am trying to fit into. It is the actual shape of my story. I started working in 2021 because there wasn't financial cushion at home, held a job through high school, and have kept working through college while carrying two majors and a 3.9. Effort was the one input available to me that didn't require money to start, and it is still the part of this I trust most.
		</p>

		<p>
			I also didn't arrive at finance through anybody's connections. I built the exposure myself, through the Introductory Finance Society, then the Tartan Student Fund, then the IB Academy, each one a door I had to go find. A program built specifically to widen access to this industry is aimed at the exact situation I have been working around for two years.
		</p>

		<p>
			The fit runs the other direction too. I taught myself to build a DCF from scratch before I had any formal training in it, which means structured technical instruction would compound on top of something rather than start from zero. What I want most from Recalc is the part I can't manufacture alone: mentors who will stress-test an assumption instead of letting a weak one slide, and a community of people running at the same thing. On the commitment the program asks for, my record is the answer. I finish what I sign up for, whether that is a fixed-date production with a 15-person crew or a chapter of 100+ that I'm now responsible for holding together.
		</p>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.intro {
		padding-block: 1rem 2rem;
	}

	.eyebrow {
		margin: 1.5rem 0 1rem;
	}

